Subject: [PATCH] detect operand-only changes (constants, stack sizes)

ghidriff does not report a function whose only change is an operand: a
bounds constant, a stack frame size, a struct offset. The function is not
listed as modified; it does not appear in the report at all.

An operand-only change is a normal shape for a security fix. MS03-026
(Blaster) is the canonical example — the patch was a stack buffer size
change.

TWO independent gates drop it, and both must be fixed; either alone still
reports nothing.

1. syms_need_diff() decides whether a matched pair is worth diffing at all,
   before anything is decompiled. It triggers only on a change in
   func.body.numAddresses or sym.referenceCount. An operand-only edit
   changes neither, so the function is skipped outright. Function byte
   LENGTH is effectively ghidriff's primary change test.

   Fixed by comparing the function's bytes when the lengths match. This is
   triage only: returning True means "look closer", and the existing diff
   decides what actually changed. Over-triggering costs time, never
   correctness — which matters for shared-cache images, where rebasing
   shifts some bytes without the code changing.

   The array handed to Memory.getBytes must be a Java byte[]. Passing a
   Python bytearray silently reads nothing and makes every function compare
   equal, which is a quiet false negative rather than an error.

2. The 'code' diff type is then gated on

       if len(only_code_diff) > 0 and (mnemonics_ratio < 1.0 or blocks_ratio < 1.0):

   `instructions` is str(code_unit) and keeps operands ("cmp x0, #0x40");
   `mnemonics` is getMnemonicString() and drops them ("cmp"). An
   operand-only edit leaves mnemonics_ratio and blocks_ratio at 1.0, so
   'code' is never appended and the function is discarded again by the
   `if len(diff_type) == 0: continue` below. instructions_ratio is computed
   on the preceding line, is < 1.0 for exactly these cases, and is already
   carried out as 'i_ratio' — it simply is not consulted.

   THE OMISSION IS DELIBERATE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. All three streams are built
   together in the same loop (ghidra_diff_engine.py ~line 437):

       instructions.append(str(code))                  # keeps operands
       mnemonics.append(str(code.getMnemonicString())) # drops operands
       ...
       blocks.extend(sorted(units))   # "sort - This case handles the case
                                      #  for compiler optimizations"

   The gate is built from exactly the two operand-free signals, and blocks is
   additionally sorted so that reordering within a basic block does not count
   either. That comment is the author stating the intent outright: the trigger
   is meant to survive a recompile. i_ratio is computed, reported in the
   markdown table, and never used to decide anything — it is a diagnostic.

   Which makes this a specificity-for-sensitivity trade, and the measurement
   below says the trade is correct for cache-resident input and wrong only for
   rebase-free input. So instructions_ratio must go behind an opt-in flag
   rather than into the default gate.

Measured with scripts/ghidriff-blindspot-test.sh (ghidriff 1.x, Ghidra 12.2,
--min-func-len 1, arm64 macOS; SimpleDiff and the default engine agree):

    case     same __text size   before   after
    base     yes                0        0     control, must stay 0
    const    yes                0        2     n > 64 -> n > 32
    stack    yes                0        2     buf[256] -> buf[128]
    struct   no                 1        1     added if statement

After the fix _check is reported with diff_type ['code'] and i_ratio 0.97
(const) / 0.74 (stack). The second reported function is the C runtime entry
stub, which also shifts; triage over-reporting, not a miss.

Applying only hunk 1 still yields 0 — the pair reaches the diff loop and is
then dropped by gate 2.

DO NOT APPLY TO SHARED-CACHE WORK AS WRITTEN — MEASURED FALSE POSITIVES
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Correct on standalone Mach-Os (the harness control stays 0; a macho keeps
__TEXT at 0x100000000 across builds, so its bytes are comparable).

It floods on dylibs and kexts pulled from a dyld_shared_cache or kernelcache,
which are rebased between builds. Measured on iPhone13,3 23G90 -> 23H128 with
this patch applied (raw numbers in ghidriff-dylib-false-positives.tsv):

  ARKit                           mod=29   of 92 functions
  ActionButtonSelector            mod=439  of 1448

Both have an IDENTICAL version string in both caches, and ARKit's normalised
instruction stream is byte-identical (329 vs 329 instructions), so every one
of those 29 is a false positive — roughly a third of the binary.

Two binaries even report more modified functions than they contain, which is
impossible for real detections and a clear tell on its own:

  AirPlayMirroringModule          mod=3218 total=346
  ProactivePredictionFoundation   mod=6723 total=828

Cause: _func_bytes_differ compares RAW bytes, and rebasing rewrites bytes in
functions whose code did not change. Gate 2 then passes as well, because
rebasing perturbs the LDR/ADD immediates that make instructions_ratio < 1.0.
Both hunks fire on the same noise. The comment in hunk 1 claiming
over-triggering "costs time, never correctness" is wrong for this input: the
downstream diff does not filter the noise back out.

A usable version has to be rebase-aware — compare a NORMALISED instruction
stream (addresses, labels and hex literals masked) rather than raw bytes,
which is what scripts/text-changed.sh does. That also inherits its limit:
a pure-constant change then becomes invisible, so masking alone cannot be
the whole answer for cache-resident binaries.

HOW MUCH IS THERE TO FIND HERE ANYWAY: ZERO, ON THIS INPUT
----------------------------------------------------------
The case for this patch was that operand-only change is a real and common
shape. On standalone Mach-Os that stands — the harness proves it, and MS03-026
is the canonical example. On cache-extracted dylibs it does not:

  40 randomly sampled changed dylibs, iOS 26.7.1 -> 26.7.2, arm64e:
      7  __TEXT.__text size changed  (real change, caught by the cheap filter)
     30  version string only, normalised code stream IDENTICAL
      0  same size with a differing instruction stream
      1  disassembly error

So there is no population of same-size code changes hiding behind ghidriff's
gate for this input. Measurements claiming otherwise (including an earlier
53% figure recorded here) came from a normaliser bug that let a zero offset
through unmasked — `ldr x0, [x8, #0x1a8]` -> `ldr x0, [x8]`, which carries no
0x prefix on one side and so escaped the mask.

Conclusion for upstream: hunk 1 should normalise rather than compare raw
bytes, hunk 2 belongs behind a flag (--operand-changes), and the flag is
worth having for filesystem machos and standalone binaries, not for
shared-cache work.

--- a/ghidriff/ghidra_diff_engine.py
+++ b/ghidriff/ghidra_diff_engine.py
@@ -1400,6 +1400,12 @@
